{"screens": {"Pell Dunmore": "Sefa opens the damp book with care, tilts it toward the light from the doorway and turns back to the last three weeks. She reads the names aloud one by one, each with its day, even where the chalk or ink is smudged. Her eyes stay mostly on the pages, with a glance now and then at Hale. When she finishes she closes the book without tying it. She says these are the men owed, three weeks as Hale said, and that she will copy them as they stand so Thorn can't claim they guessed. Then she looks toward the boarded part of the floor and asks Hale what was burning in the brazier, since the ash is wrong for a fire of shavings.

You stay on the threshold with your boots on the white chalk and breathe through your nose as little as you can. The sweet smell is faint, like bread left too long in a cold oven.

You run your eyes over the room the way you'd look over a strongroom: the brazier first, then the flags around it, then the boarded back third. You are after the small things that tell you what a room is really for.", "together": [["Pell Dunmore", "Sefa Marrin"]], "places": {"Pell Dunmore": "on the chalk threshold of the room, beside Hale", "Sefa Marrin": "inside the room, near the brazier"}, "waiting": {}}
